---
// Setup — the home base for standing a tenant up. Not a wizard: a set of
// MILESTONES, each its own URL, resumable, in any order its dependencies allow.
//
// The brief's five (docs/product-brief.md §5):
//
// 1. ONE QUESTION: "What is left before this tenant is stood up, and where do I
//    pick it up?" The meter answers it as one fraction, the cards answer it one
//    milestone at a time; both read the same derived statuses, so they cannot
//    disagree.
// 2. READERS: the program admin (Dana) in year zero, with a signed contract and
//    a folder of documents. Hardest to serve is her ABSENT counterpart — the six
//    staff who will meet every vocabulary she confirms here as the dropdowns on
//    their project records. Their burden is priced by the counts on each card:
//    "11 suggested organizations" is eleven names somebody will have to pick
//    from.
// 3. SERIES/SCALAR: one homogeneous set of cards in three groups ordered by
//    dependency: Describe your program (Start, Program shape, Names and
//    appearance), Define what a project is made of (Organizations, Funding
//    sources, Classifications, Project stages, Spatial areas), Bring in the work
//    (Import projects, Performance measures, People). Each group prints its count
//    and its definition of done under the heading. Andy, 2026-09-23: this
//    replaced five subject sections (Your program, Who is involved, Money, How
//    work is organized, Results), which left two groups holding one or two cards
//    and said nothing about what finished meant. The completion meter is the
//    page's only aggregate and it leads, because it is the fraction she reports
//    upward.
// 4. IRREVERSIBLE: nothing. Drafts are browser-local patches and milestone status
//    is derived, never stored, so no card gets a warning band. Reset demo is the
//    one destructive act and it is a ghost button in the bar, beside the way
//    out, not a danger zone. Complete demo is its mirror: it overwrites the
//    whole draft with a finished one, which is the intended demo semantics.
// 5. CUT: step numbers, a "next step" prompt, and a per-milestone description.
//    All three assert a sequence the model does not have. Also cut: a lede under
//    the page title, which would be text about a page that already shows its own
//    state.
//
// COMPLETE DEMO, 2026-09-23. A demo has to reach the finish line without
// walking eleven milestones, so the bar carries a second ghost control that
// writes a fully confirmed draft (fillSetupDraft). It sits before Reset demo:
// the two are a pair, fill then clear, and the layout's own way out stays last.
// When the draft reads 11 of 11 the finish appears between the title and the
// grid; Reset demo takes it away through the same `setup-draft-change` event.
//
// THE FINISH IS INLINE, 2026-09-23. ANDY REDIRECTED THE FINISH THE SAME DAY IT
// WAS BUILT: NO SEPARATE PAGE. /prototypes/setup/complete AND ITS THREE
// COMPONENTS WERE REMOVED, AND THE BAND THAT LINKED THERE BECAME
// firma2-setup-finish, WHICH READS THE PROGRAM BACK HERE AS ONE PARAGRAPH IN
// LARGE TYPE, EACH FACT A TOKEN IN ITS MILESTONE'S COLOUR AND ICON, WITH THE
// NEXT STEPS THAT HAVE A SCREEN AS BUTTONS UNDER IT.
//
// The setup layer (bar + centred column) comes from SetupLayout.
import SetupLayout from '../../../layouts/SetupLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import Firma2SetupProgress from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-progress.astro';
import Firma2SetupMilestoneGrid from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-milestone-grid.astro';
import Firma2SetupFinish from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-finish.astro';
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: stack(2xl)
  sections:
    - bar actions -> SetupLayout bar slot      (Complete demo, then Reset demo)
    - page header -> firma2-page-header
      - progress    -> firma2-setup-progress   (actions slot: fraction and sash)
    - finish      -> firma2-setup-finish       (only at 11 of 11: the program as one paragraph of tokens, next-step buttons)
    - milestones  -> firma2-setup-milestone-grid  (three groups: heading, count and definition of done, cards)
-->
<SetupLayout title="Setup">
  <!-- The seam for re-running the demo, in the bar beside the way out. Ghost,
       because clearing a browser-local draft is not the page's workflow — it is
       the way back to the start of one. data-setup-reset lands on the lego's
       inner native button (esa-button forwards rest props to it), so the
       handler below is delegated on the document rather than bound to this
       element. Complete demo is the same shape with data-setup-fill: the way
       to the end of the workflow for a demo that cannot spend the time walking
       it. -->
  <EsaButton slot="bar" variant="ghost" size="sm" data-setup-fill>Complete demo</EsaButton>
  <EsaButton slot="bar" variant="ghost" size="sm" data-setup-reset>Reset demo</EsaButton>

  <div class="stack" data-gap="2xl">
    {/* The meter rides the title line: one row reads Setup, the fraction, the
        sash. It is the page's only aggregate and it is what the h1 is about. */}
    <Firma2PageHeader title="Setup">
      <Firma2SetupProgress slot="actions" />
    </Firma2PageHeader>

    {/* Hidden until every milestone is confirmed; its own script decides. */}
    <Firma2SetupFinish />

    <Firma2SetupMilestoneGrid />

  </div>
</SetupLayout>

<script>
  import { clearSetupDraft, fillSetupDraft } from '../../../lib/setup-draft';

  // Both writers dispatch `setup-draft-change`, so the meter, the cards and the
  // finish re-derive themselves. Nothing here touches a section directly.
  document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    const target = event.target as HTMLElement | null;
    if (target?.closest('[data-setup-fill]')) fillSetupDraft();
    else if (target?.closest('[data-setup-reset]')) clearSetupDraft();
  });
</script>
